toggles visibility. I need to be careful about the order of adding listeners to avoid infinite loops when the confirmation field's text changes programmatically. The issue is that modifying the transformation method doesn't actually trigger a TextWatcher event, so I need a different strategy. I'll set up the password toggle on the TextInputLayout and use a TextWatcher to sync the visibility state between the two fields when the user interacts with the toggle. The simpler approach is to just check the password field's transformation method directly when it gains focus. When either field receives focus, I'll detect whether it's currently showing the password in plain text or masked, then apply the same visibility state to the other field. This avoids the complexity of tracking state changes or trying to hook into the password toggle's internal behavior. I see that the password toggle listener only fires when the eye icon is clicked, not when the user navigates via focus. I could add a TextWatcher on the password field that checks `isPasswordVisible()` and mirrors the visibility state to the confirmation field, but this creates a complication—the confirmation field is just a plain EditText without its own toggle mechanism. I need to reconsider the approach since syncing via TextWatcher would require the confirmation field to also support the same visibility toggle behavior.\n\nLooking at the Material Design source, the `passwordToggleChecked` state should remain true even after toggling visibility, which means I can use it as a signal to sync the confirmation field's transformation method whenever the toggle state changes.\n\nI'll wrap the confirmation field in a `TextInputLayout` with its own toggle enabled, then add a listener to sync both toggle states so they stay in sync when the user clicks either eye icon.\n\nFor the first-run setup, I'll need to modify the layout XML to wrap `introPasswordConfirm` in a `TextInputLayout` and update the Java code to handle the dual-field toggle synchronization.\n\nNow I'm checking the other affected files—`component_password.xml` and `CredentialsPreference.java` both follow the same pattern, while `component_authentication.xml` and `PasswordEncryptedPreference.java` use an identical structure. The `dialog_password_entry.xml` layout has the same issue with `passwordConfirm` as a plain EditText, and `PasswordEntryDialog.java` already has a TextWatcher in place. I'll apply the same fix across all four locations.\n\nNow I need to verify that `PlainTextTransformationMethod` is available in AndroidX. Since the project already uses `TextInputEditText` from the Material library, the AndroidX Material dependency should include support for this transformation method, which was added in API 28+.\n\nLooking at the `build.gradle` to confirm the AndroidX setup...\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
