en/activity_margin\"\n        android:gravity=\"end\" >\n\n        <Button\n            android:id=\"@+id/btnCancel\"\n            android:layout_width=\"wrap_content\"\n            android:layout_height=\"wrap_content\"\n            style=\"?android:attr/buttonBarButtonStyle\"\n            android:text=\"@string/button_cancel\" />\n\n        <Button\n            android:id=\"@+id/btnSave\"\n            android:layout_width=\"wrap_content\"\n            android:layout_height=\"wrap_content\"\n            style=\"?android:attr/buttonBarButtonStyle\"\n            android:text=\"@string/button_save\" />\n\n    </androidx.appcompat.widget.ButtonBarLayout>\n\n</LinearLayout>"}]}}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"e19c37d5-f27f-4fc6-b82e-eb6e58bef109","session_id":"4163bdfc-3a81-4fb8-be42-5517abb2c26a","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"e19c37d5-f27f-4fc6-b82e-eb6e58bef109","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I also need to check if there's a `PasswordEncryptedPreference.java`. Let me also check the build.gradle to see what Android libraries are available.\n\nBut first, let me formulate the complete fix. I've identified all four locations that need fixing:\n\n1. **IntroScreenActivity.java** + **component_intro_authentication.xml** - First-time password setup\n2. **CredentialsPreference.java** + **component_authentication.xml** - Settings auth method change\n3. **PasswordEncryptedPreference.java** + **component_password.xml** - Backup password change\n\n\n4. **PasswordEntryDialog.java** + **dialog_password_entry.xml** - Backup restore password entry\n\nThe core problem is that confirm password fields are plain `EditText` widgets without visibility toggles, so when users toggle the primary password visibility, the confirm field stays hidden. I'm going to wrap each confirm field in a `TextInputLayout` with `passwordToggleEnabled` to give it its own toggle, then add a `TextWatcher` to sync the toggle states between both fields.\n\nWait, I'm realizing I need to reconsider the approach here—the real issue is ensuring both password fields stay synchronized when toggling visibility, not just adding toggles to both. I'm realizing the issue with the TextWatcher approach—if the confirmation field has a TextWatcher that modifies the password field's transformation, it could create a circular problem. When the eye icon toggles visibility on the password field, the TextWatcher fires, which then tries to update the confirmation field, potentially triggering another cascade of events.\n\nThe real problem is that `setTransformationMethod()` doesn't directly trigger a TextWatcher, but I need to verify whether the password toggle mechanism somehow modifies the text itself before the TextWatcher fires. Let me reconsider the Material Design source—the password toggle should only call `setTextFilter()` and `toggleEndIconDelegate()`, not change the actual text content. I'm realizing the TextWatcher approach won't work because `setTransformationMethod` doesn't trigger `afterTextChanged`. Let me reconsider what the Material Design library actually does when toggling password visibility—it must be modifying the text field in a way that could be observed. I need to find a way to sync the visibility state between the password fields without relying on TextWatcher callbacks. I'm realizing the TextWatcher won't actually fire when the password visibility toggles, since it only triggers on text changes. Let me consider using a ViewTreeObserver to monitor when the view hierarchy updates instead—wrapping the confirm field in TextInputLayout and detecting layout changes should let me sync the visibility states properly. I'm running into the same issue here—`isPasswordVisible` doesn't actually change when the text filter is applied. I need a different approach to detect when the password is being revealed. Let me reconsider the API: Material's `passwordToggleEnabled` toggles between an eye icon and a text filter that shows plain text, but I need to find a reliable way to track whether the password is currently visible or masked. I'm realizing that modifying the `hint` field on the delegating view doesn't actually trigger any observable change since it's just a reference, not the password text itself. The `setTextFilter()` method is where the actual visibility toggling happens, so I need a different approach to detect when the user clicks the password toggle icon.\n\nLet me think through whether there's a simpler solution by examining the actual implementation more carefully.\n\nI've narrowed down to three viable options: using an AccessibilityNodeInfoProvider to detect password visibility changes, wrapping the confirm field in its own TextInputLayout with passwordToggleEnabled to give users individual toggle controls, or trying to hook into the end icon click with a ViewTreeObserver. The TextInputLayout approach seems most straightforward—I'd need to update the layout XML to wrap the confirm field and then add the corresponding Java code to handle the toggle behavior.\n\nFor the Java side, I'd sync the password visibility state between the two text fields using a TextWatcher that checks isPasswordVisible() and applies the appropriate transformation method accordingly. The key is making sure the TextWatcher on the primary field mirrors its visibility state to the confirm field whenever it changes. When the TextWatcher on passwordInput triggers, it calls `isPasswordVisible()` and syncs that state to passwordConfirm. Since passwordConfirm doesn't have its own TextWatcher in this setup, there's no risk of creating a loop—the visibility change happens through setTransformationMethod(), which won't trigger any TextWatcher callbacks anyway.\n\nThe approach is clean: I only need to add the TextWatcher to passwordInput and let it update passwordConfirm's transformation method without any feedback loops.\n\nNow I'm looking at applying this same fix across all four locations in the codebase, starting with IntroScreenActivity and its corresponding XML file. I see the issue now—the confirm field has `android:inputType=\"textPassword\"` which masks the input, but unlike the primary password field, it doesn't have a `TextInputLayout` wrapper with `passwordToggleEnabled` to let users toggle visibility. The toggle feature is only available on the first field, so the confirm field stays masked even when the user wants to see what they're typing.\n\nThe best fix is to wrap the confirm field in its own `TextInputLayout` with `passwordToggleEnabled` so both fields get independent visibility toggles, and then sync them with a `TextWatcher`. Let me check if the project uses AndroidX...\n\nChecking the imports... I can see `androidx.appcompat` is being used, which means this is an AndroidX project. Now I'm mapping out all the layout files that need updating and identifying which Java classes require the TextWatcher sync logic to keep both password fields in sync.\n\nI should also look at `PasswordEncryptedPreference.java` since it likely follows the same pattern. Let me start implementing these changes and then I'll build and test to verify everything works.\n\nNow I'm writing the modified XML layouts, starting with wrapping the password confirmation fields in TextInputLayout wrappers and enabling the password toggle feature for each one. Writing XML layout code... Setting up the TextInputLayout with password toggle enabled and configuring the password input field with appropriate constraints, then adding a confirmation field below it.\n\nNow I'm setting up the AuthenticationFragment class with its member variables...\n\nI need to find the password confirmation layout element, then add a text change listener to sync password visibility between the two input fields. When the user toggles password visibility on the main field, it should update the confirmation field accordingly, using the transformation method to switch between masked and plain text display. I need to add a TextWatcher to sync the password visibility between the two input fields, which means importing PlainTextTransformationMethod. However, I'm realizing that TextWatcher callbacks happen when the text content changes, not when the password visibility toggle fires, so this approach won't actually detect visibility state changes.\n\nLooking at the TextInputLayout source code, the password toggle visibility is controlled by the textFilter property, which gets set to null to show the password or to an Editable filter to hide it.\n\nWhen the filter changes, `getText()` returns the current text whether it's visible or hidden, and the EditText's tr