{"type":"user","uuid":"8e511fad-b326-4510-a9c0-6e513b71a0d0","session_id":"4163bdfc-3a81-4fb8-be42-5517abb2c26a","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e3495e6222f03c3","is_error":false,"content":"/*\n * Copyright (C) 2017-2018 Jakob Nixdorf\n *\n * Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\n * of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\n * in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\n * to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\n * copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\n * furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n *\n * The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all\n * copies or substantial portions of the Software.\n *\n * THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\n * IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\n * FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\n * AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\n * LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\n * OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\n * SOFTWARE.\n */\n\npackage org.shadowice.flocke.andotp.Preferences;\n\nimport android.app.AlertDialog;\nimport android.content.Context;\nimport android.content.res.TypedArray;\nimport android.preference.DialogPreference;\nimport com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText;\nimport com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout;\nimport android.text.Editable;\nimport android.text.InputType;\nimport android.text.TextWatcher;\nimport android.text.method.PasswordTransformationMethod;\nimport android.util.AttributeSet;\nimport android.util.Base64;\nimport android.view.View;\nimport android.widget.Button;\nimport android.widget.EditText;\n\nimport org.shadowice.flocke.andotp.R;\nimport org.shadowice.flocke.andotp.Utilities.Constants;\nimport org.shadowice.flocke.andotp.Utilities.EncryptionHelper;\nimport org.shadowice.flocke.andotp.Utilities.KeyStoreHelper;\nimport org.shadowice.flocke.andotp.Utilities.Settings;\n\nimport java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;\nimport java.security.KeyPair;\n\npublic class PasswordEncryptedPreference extends DialogPreference\n    implements View.OnClickListener, TextWatcher {\n\n    public enum Mode {\n        PASSWORD, PIN\n    }\n\n    private KeyPair key;\n\n    private static final String DEFAULT_VALUE = \"\";\n\n    private Mode mode = Mode.PASSWORD;\n\n    private TextInputEditText passwordInput;\n    private EditText passwordConfirm;\n\n    private Button btnSave;\n\n    private String value = DEFAULT_VALUE;\n\n    public PasswordEncryptedPreference(Context context, AttributeSet attrs) {\n        super(context, attrs);\n\n        try {\n            key = KeyStoreHelper.loadOrGenerateAsymmetricKeyPair(context, Constants.KEYSTORE_ALIAS_PASSWORD);\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n\n        setDialogLayoutResource(R.layout.component_password);\n    }\n\n    public void setMode(Mode mode) {\n        this.mode = mode;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected void onPrepareDialogBuilder(AlertDialog.Builder builder) {\n        super.onPrepareDialogBuilder(builder);\n\n        builder.setPositiveButton(null, null);\n        builder.setNegativeButton(null, null);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected void onBindDialogView(View view) {\n        Settings settings = new Settings(getContext());\n\n        TextInputLayout passwordLayout = view.findViewById(R.id.passwordLayout);\n        passwordInput = view.findViewById(R.id.passwordEdit);\n        passwordConfirm = view.findViewById(R.id.passwordConfirm);\n\n        if (settings.getBlockAccessibility()) {\n            passwordLayout.setImportantForAccessibility(View.IMPORTANT_FOR_ACCESSIBILITY_NO_HIDE_DESCENDANTS);\n            passwordConfirm.setImportantForAccessibility(View.IMPORTANT_FOR_ACCESSIBILITY_NO);\n        }\n\n        Button btnCancel = view.findViewById(R.id.btnCancel);\n        btnSave = view.findViewById(R.id.btnSave);\n        btnSave.setEnabled(false);\n\n        btnCancel.setOnClickListener(this);\n        btnSave.setOnClickListener(this);\n\n        if (! value.isEmpty()) {\n            passwordInput.setText(value);\n        }\n\n        if (mode == Mode.PASSWORD) {\n            passwordLayout.setHint(getContext().getString(R.string.settings_hint_password));\n            passwordConfirm.setHint(R.string.settings_hint_password_confirm);\n\n            passwordInput.setInputType(InputType.TYPE_CLASS_TEXT | InputType.TYPE_TEXT_VARIATION_PASSWORD);\n            passwordConfirm.setInputType(InputType.TYPE_CLASS_TEXT | InputType.TYPE_TEXT_VARIATION_PASSWORD);\n        } else if (mode == Mode.PIN) {\n            passwordLayout.setHint(getContext().getString(R.string.settings_hint_pin));\n            passwordConfirm.setHint(R.string.settings_hint_pin_confirm);\n\n            passwordInput.setInputType(InputType.TYPE_CLASS_NUMBER | InputType.TYPE_NUMBER_VARIATION_PASSWORD);\n            passwordConfirm.setInputType(InputType.TYPE_CLASS_NUMBER | InputType.TYPE_NUMBER_VARIATION_PASSWORD);\n        }\n\n        passwordInput.setTransformationMethod(new PasswordTransformationMethod());\n        passwordConfirm.setTransformationMethod(new PasswordTransformationMethod());\n\n        passwordConfirm.addTextChangedListener(this);\n        passwordInput.addTextChangedListener(this);\n\n        super.onBindDialogView(view);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected Object onGetDefaultValue(TypedArray a, int index) {\n        return a.getString(index);\n    }\n\n    private void encryptAndPersist(String value) {\n        try {\n            byte[] encBytes = EncryptionHelper.encrypt(key.getPublic(), value.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));\n            persistString(Base64.encodeToString(encBytes, Base64.URL_SAFE));\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n    }\n\n    private void restoreAndDecrypt(String encValue) {\n        try {\n            byte[] encBytes = Base64.decode(encValue, Base64.URL_SAFE);\n            value = new String(EncryptionHelper.decrypt(key.getPrivate(), encBytes), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected void onSetInitialValue(boolean restorePersistedValue, Object defaultValue) {\n        if (restorePersistedValue) {\n            restoreAndDecrypt(getPersistedString(DEFAULT_VALUE));\n        } else {\n            value = (String) defaultValue;\n            encryptAndPersist(value);\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onClick(View view) {\n        switch (view.getId()) {\n            case (R.id.btnCancel):\n                getDialog().dismiss();\n                break;\n            case (R.id.btnSave):\n                value = passwordInput.getText().toString();\n                encryptAndPersist(value);\n\n                getDialog().dismiss();\n                break;\n            default:\n                break;\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onTextChanged(CharSequence s, int start, int before, int count) {\n        if (passwordConfirm.getEditableText().toString().equals(passwordInput.getEditableText().toString())) {\n            btnSave.setEnabled(true);\n        } else {\n            btnSave.setEnabled(false);\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void afterTextChanged(Editable s) {}\n\n    @Override\n    public void beforeTextChanged(CharSequence s, int start, int count, int after) {}\n}\n"}]}}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"8e84aa73-5548-4911-8112-082b5973cfc0","session_id":"4163bdfc-3a81-4fb8-be42-5517abb2c26a","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"8e84aa73-5548-4911-8112-082b5973cfc0","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me summarize:\n\n**minSdkVersion is 19**, so `PlainTextTransformationMethod` (API 28+) is NOT available.\n\nI need a different approach. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nSince `PlainTextTransformationMethod` isn't available for minSdk 19, I have these options:\n\n1. **Add visibility toggle to confirm field via TextInputLayout** - This gives each field its own toggle, no sync needed. The user can tap the eye icon on whichever field they want to see.\n   \n2. **Use `TransformationMethod` that shows text without masking** - This would require creating a custom class.\n \n3. **Use reflection to access Material's internal toggle detection** - Fragile and risky.\n\n4. **Create a simple custom transformation method inline** - I could implement `TransformationMethod` to return the text without masking.\n\nThe cleanest approach is option 1: wrap each confirm field in `TextInputLayout` with `app:passwordToggleEnabled=\"true\"`, and add a `TextWatcher` to the primary password field that syncs visibility. Since minSdk is 19, Material library must handle the visibility toggle for older devices.\n\nI need to find a way to detect when the Material Design password toggle activates the \"show password\" feature so I can apply the same state to the confirm field. The challenge is that changing the transformation method won't trigger the TextWatcher, and `isPasswordVisible` relies on `passwordToggleChecked` which doesn't properly reflect the actual visibility state. I'm wondering if the text filter itself changes when the toggle fires—when `textFilter` is null, the full text should be visible, but when it's set to a filter, the visibility might be restricted.\n\nThe real issue is that `TextInputEditText` overrides `getText()` to return filtered results when a filter is active, so I can't reliably detect visibility changes from within the TextWatcher. Even if I tried adding the TextWatcher with `fireImmediately=true` and checking `isPasswordVisible()` in `beforeTextChanged()`, the state wouldn't actually change until after the filter is applied.\n\nI'm realizing I could use a `View.OnFocusChangeListener` instead—when the user taps the eye icon or navigates between fields, the focus change would give me a reliable signal to check and sync the visibility state. But there's still the problem that tapping the toggle icon fires before the state actually updates, so I'd still be reading stale information.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach might be to just give each password field its own toggle button wrapped in a `TextInputLayout` with `passwordToggleEnabled`, so users can independently show or hide each field without needing to sync them. Then I can add logic to handle the save button state based on whether the fields match.\n\nBut wait, if the goal is really to sync them when the user toggles one, I need to find where Material Design actually fires that toggle event—let me dig into the `EndIconDelegate` source to see if there's a callback I can hook into.\n\nAfter looking at the code, I don't see an exposed callback for when the toggle is clicked. So I'm going to go with the simpler approach: add independent toggles to both fields and let users control each one separately. That's actually more intuitive than auto-syncing.\n\nNow I'm working through the implementation. For the layout XML, I need to wrap each `EditText` in a `TextInputLayout` and set `passwordToggleEnabled=\"true\"`. I should keep the existing IDs and move `android:inputType=\"textPassword\"` into the code where I set the transformation method, since the Material library doesn't enforce that on a plain `EditText`. For the Java code, I'll make sure both input fields are configured consistently with `PasswordTransformationMethod` applied in the code rather than relying on XML. Now I'm setting up the TextInputLayout wrapper around the confirmation password field with its own toggle enabled.\n\nI'll add a TextWatcher that syncs the password visibility state between the main input and confirmation fields, so when one toggles visibility, the other follows. I need to apply this same visibility sync pattern to IntroScreenActivity as well.\n\nNow I'm setting up the same synchronization logic in PasswordEncryptedPreference...\n\nContinuing with the implementation...\n\nActually, I'm realizing the TextWatcher approach won't catch visibility toggle clicks since those don't trigger text changes—I need a different mechanism to detect when the eye icon is tapped and propagate that state change to both fields. I'm testing whether the text content actually changes when `setTextFilter()` is called—maybe the filter mutates the text or something gets triggered. Looking at the Material source more carefully, I think I'm misunderstanding how the password toggle mechanism works, so let me reconsider the approach from scratch.\n\nThe core issue is that the Material password toggle modifies an internal text filter which affects what the EditText displays, but it doesn't change the underlying text value or trigger any callbacks I can hook into. I should either give each field its own toggle independently, or use a different event mechanism to detect when the toggle is pressed.\n\nViewTreeObserver.OnGlobalLayoutListener won't work since no layout changes occur. Focus change listeners might be better though—when the toggle icon is clicked, focus moves to that icon, so I could detect that transition and sync the visibility state on focus lost from the password field. But that feels fragile. Let me reconsider whether the TextWatcher approach could actually work here. The TextWatcher on the confirm field fires after the primary password's change, so `isPasswordVisible()` returns true and I try to sync the confirm field's transformation—but that won't trigger another TextWatcher since the confirm field's text hasn't actually changed. So when the toggle fires, the primary password's text gets replaced with the real value, TextWatcher runs and detects visibility is now true, then applies the plain text transformation to the confirm field. No cascade happens because the confirm field itself doesn't get its text modified.\n\nI need to verify this by checking what TextInputEditText.getText() actually returns. When the password is masked, it returns the filtered text (asterisks), but when the toggle is active and the filter is null, it returns the actual unfiltered text. So the visible text content should change between these states.\n\nThe key question is whether TextWatcher fires when the text becomes visible through the toggle—if the returned text changes from asterisks to the actual value, the listener should trigger.\n\nBut I need to be careful here. The asterisks aren't really in the buffer; they're rendered by the PasswordTransformationMethod masking the underlying text. So when `setTextFilter()` is called, it's not actually replacing the text in the buffer—it's setting a flag that makes the view display differently. This means `replace()` might not be the right mechanism to detect visibility changes.\n\nThe real issue is that `getText()` returns different things depending on whether a filter is set, but the underlying buffer stays the same. When I modify text in `beforeTextChanged()`, I'm modifying the buffer, not the filter. So I can't rely on TextWatcher to detect the visibility toggle—it just won't work.\n\nI'll go with the simpler approach: wrap the confirm field in its own TextInputLayout with password toggle enabled. I'm considering whether `isPasswordVisible()` might work differently than I initially thought—maybe it's not just checking `passwordToggleChecked` but also looking at the transformation method. Let me verify this against the Material library source. Looking at the actual `isPasswordVisible()` implementation, it checks whether the end icon delegate is toggled and the filter is null, which means it would return true when the toggle is clicked. This confirms the TextWatcher approach should work.\n\nNow I need to verify that `EditText.getText()` actually returns the password text when the filter is null—looking at the source, I see there's a `transformedText` field being managed internally, which suggests the behavior might be more complex than a simple null check.\n\nLet me trace through the Material library's implementation: when `setTextFilter(null)` is called, it clears the filter and should display the actual text, but I want to confirm this works correctly across different scenarios rather than making assumptions based on API differences.\n\nI'm going to implement the fix directly—wrapping the confirm field in a TextInputLayout and adding a TextWatcher to sync the password visibility states—then test it to see if it works. If the TextWatcher doesn't fire when toggling, I can fall back to using ViewTreeObserver or FocusChange listeners instead.\n\nWriting the implementation... I'm noticing a potential recursion issue when the confirm field's TextWatcher modifies the password input's transformation method—when I call `setTransformationMethod()`, it should trigger an internal text change that fires the listener back on passwordInput, creating a cycle.\n\nHowever, since the actual text values haven't changed, the `onTextChanged()` callback will see identical sequences and the equality check will pass, so the recursion terminates naturally without causing problems.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement this across all four locations. I should also add the TextWatcher to PasswordEntryDialog.java, though I need to check how it currently handles the confirm field—specifically whether `passwordInput.getEditableText()` might be null in certain modes, and whether the TextWatcher gets properly removed when the dialog closes.\n\nFor the dialog modes, when the confirm field is hidden in ENTER mode, I won't need to sync passwords there, but in UPDATE mode I'll need to add the TextWatcher to handle the visibility syncing. I'm noticing the current `onTextChanged` implementation might be fragile with its null handling.\n\nI should use the `fireImmediately` parameter when adding the TextWatcher to passwordInput, since setting the transformation method programmatically triggers a TextChanged event—and I need that callback to fire","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"518bbcc3-e3ca-4786-8da6-39a18f700f83","session_id":"4163bdfc-3a81-4fb8-be42-5517abb2c26a","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"518bbcc3-e3ca-4786-8da6-39a18f700f83","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have a complete understanding of the bug and all affected files. Let me summarize:\n\n**Root cause:** The confirm password field (`EditText`) has `android:inputType=\"textPassword\"` which always masks input. The primary password field uses `TextInputLayout` with `app:passwordToggleEnabled=\"true\"`, but when the eye icon is tapped, only the primary field's text becomes visible—the confirm field's text remains masked because it has no visibility toggle and no sync mechanism.\n\n**Affected locations (4 pairs of layout + Java files):**\n1. `IntroScreenActivity.java` + `component_intro_authentication.xml` (first-time setup)\n2. `CredentialsPreference.java` + `component_authentication.xml` (settings auth change)\n3. `PasswordEncryptedPreference.java` + `component_password.xml` (backup password change)\n4. `PasswordEntryDialog.java` + `dialog_password_entry.xml` (backup restore)\n\n**Fix:** Add a `TextWatcher` to the primary password field that syncs the `PasswordTransformationMethod` ↔ `PlainTextTransformationMethod` state to the confirm field. This way, whenever the Material Design toggle reveals/hides the primary field's text, the confirm field follows.\n\nLet me now implement all the fixes:\n\n\n\n\n"}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
